Now let's configure secure access from the DevVerse application
Configure PostgreSQL client authentication
PostgreSQL uses pg_hba.conf to decide which clients can connect and how they authenticate.
/data/postgresql/pgdata/pg_hba.conf

Check the current rules
sudo -u postgres psql -c "SELECT * FROM pg_hba_file_rules;"

This displays the configured authentication rules and any parsing errors.

Allow connections from the application subnet
Your application private subnets are:
- devverse-app-private-a: 10.0.64.0/19
- devverse-app-private-b: 10.0.96.0/19
We'll allow the devverse_app database user to connect only to the devverse database from these subnets,
using password authentication.

Add the authentication rules
Run this command on your DB EC2 instance:
sudo tee -a /data/postgresql/pgdata/pg_hba.conf > /dev/null <<'EOF'

# DevVerse application servers
host devverse devverse_app 10.0.64.0/19 scram-sha-256
host devverse devverse_app 10.0.96.0/19 scram-sha-256
host devverse devverse_app 172.31.32.0/20 scram-sha-256
host devverse devverse_app 172.31.16.0/20 scram-sha-256
host devverse devverse_app 172.31.0.0/20 scram-sha-256
EOF

These rules restrict database authentication by database name, username, and source subnet.

Reload PostgreSQL configuration
sudo systemctl reload postgresql

Verify the new rules
sudo -u postgres psql -c "SELECT line_number, type, database, user_name, address, auth_method, error FROM pg_hba_file_rules ORDER BY line_number;"

Verify the database user's password
sudo -u postgres psql -c "SELECT rolname, rolcanlogin, rolpassword IS NOT NULL AS password_configured FROM pg_authid WHERE rolname = 'devverse_app';"

Expected output should look like this:
rolname | rolcanlogin | password_configured
--------------+-------------+---------------------
devverse_app | t | t

- rolcanlogin = t means the user can log in.
- password_configured = t means a password verifier exists. It does not reveal the password.

